\description{
\code{be_assess()} performs a complete bioequivalence (BE) assessment of one or
more noncompartmental endpoints against a regulatory framework, including
reference scaling and the pass/fail decision.  It accepts the results of
\code{\link[=pk.nca]{pk.nca()}} directly (a \code{PKNCAresults} object) or a tidy long data.frame, so
the same workflow that produces NCA parameters flows into the regulatory
decision.  It is a thin wrapper over \code{\link[=be_fit_models]{be_fit_models()}} that adds the
\code{be_assess} class and its print/summary methods.
}
\details{
The model type is selected automatically from the design and the regulator.
By design, a parallel study (one measurement per subject) uses a fixed-effects
ANOVA (\code{"anova"}), while a crossover or replicate study (repeated measures per
subject) uses the mixed model (\code{"lmer"}) for the geometric mean ratio and its
confidence interval.  The FDA reference-scaled frameworks (FDA RSABE, NTID,
HVNTID) always use intra-subject contrasts (\code{"isc"}), as the guidances
specify, regardless of design.  Each subject's contrast is its mean log test
minus its mean log reference, from the subjects with every test and
reference administration their sequence calls for; the ratio is the
unweighted mean of the sequence means of the contrasts, with \code{n - m} degrees
of freedom for \code{n} subjects in \code{m} sequences (FDA draft guidances on
progesterone and warfarin sodium).  Without a sequence column, a subject's
sequence is its treatment-by-period pattern.  The unscaled 80.00-125.00\%
criterion of these frameworks (FDA RSABE when \code{swR < 0.294}, NTID, and
HVNTID) uses the model of \code{model_type}; the default \code{"isc"} is the guidance
mixed-effects model for replicate designs: fixed sequence, period, and
treatment effects, correlated treatment random effects for each subject, a
residual variance per treatment, REML, and Satterthwaite degrees of freedom
(SAS \verb{RANDOM TRT/TYPE=FA0(2)}, \code{REPEATED/GRP=TRT}).  A formulation that no
subject receives twice has its residual variance folded into its
between-subject variance, where the guidance model is over-specified, and a
subject missing a period forms its own sequence when the sequence is derived
from the treatment pattern.  When the mixed model cannot be fit (for
example, a formulation whose replicates agree exactly), only a decision that
needs the unscaled criterion stops with an error.  Each row shows the ratio
and interval its decision used: the intra-subject contrasts for the
reference-scaled criterion and the model for the unscaled one, and \code{n} counts
the subjects behind that ratio: for the intra-subject contrasts, the subjects
with every test and reference administration their sequence calls for; for
the model, every subject with the test or the reference formulation.  Pass
\code{model_type} to override (\code{"lmer"}, \code{"anova"}, \code{"isc"}, \code{"nlme"}, or
\code{"gls"}); with \code{"isc"} the FDA mixed model also gives the ratio for the
other frameworks, and a design without a replicated formulation keeps the
fixed-effects ANOVA ratio.  Within-subject
variability for the FDA frameworks uses the FDA estimator from within-sequence
differences between replicate administrations (\code{model_type = "isc"} in
\code{\link[=be_within_var]{be_within_var()}}); for the other frameworks it uses the EMA ANOVA estimator
for the \code{"lmer"}/\code{"anova"}/\code{"isc"} paths and the treatment-specific
mixed-model estimator for \code{"nlme"}.

With \code{heteroscedastic = TRUE} the residual variance is estimated separately
for each treatment: a crossover or replicate study uses \code{nlme::lme()} with
\code{varIdent(~ 1 | treatment)} (\code{"nlme"}), and a parallel study uses
\code{nlme::gls()} with the same variance structure (\code{"gls"}).  \code{lme4::lmer()}
cannot estimate treatment-specific residual variances (its \code{weights} are
fixed prior weights), so \code{heteroscedastic = TRUE} with \code{model_type = "lmer"}
is an error.  Without reference scaling, \code{"nlme"} accepts non-replicated
designs and several test formulations; with reference scaling it needs a
fully replicated design and one test formulation, because the
per-formulation variances are then used as the within-subject variances.

\code{regulator = "descriptive"} estimates the geometric means, their ratio, and
its confidence interval exactly as for \code{"ABE"} but applies no acceptance
limits and reports no pass/fail, for comparisons such as food effect and
drug-drug interaction studies.

The expanding-limits frameworks widen only some endpoints, as their
guidances specify (see \code{\link[=be_regulator]{be_regulator()}} for the sources): the EMA and the GCC
widen Cmax, and Health Canada widens AUC and judges Cmax by its point
estimate alone.  Every other endpoint needs its confidence interval within
80.00-125.00\%.  The \code{rule} column names the criterion each row used.
\code{widen_endpoints} adds endpoints to those that widen, for example
\code{"ctrough"} for Ctau,ss of a modified-release product under the EMA
modified-release guideline.  \code{widen_default = FALSE} drops the framework's
own choice, for example when the protocol did not specify widening in
advance, which the EMA and the GCC require; the endpoints in
\code{widen_endpoints} still widen.  Health Canada judges Cmax by its point
estimate unless Cmax is named in \code{widen_endpoints}.

For the results of a sparse bootstrap (Shen and Machado 2017; see
\code{\link[=sparse_bootstrap]{sparse_bootstrap()}}), there is one estimate per treatment, from the sparse
data, rather than one per subject.  The ratio of the test and reference
estimates is the point estimate, and its confidence interval is the
percentile interval of the ratios of the bootstrap replicates (\code{model_type = "bootstrap"}).  The \verb{gm_*} columns hold the estimates of each treatment with
their percentile intervals.  A parallel design resamples the treatments
independently; for a crossover, give the treatment column as \code{paired} to
\code{\link[=sparse_bootstrap]{sparse_bootstrap()}}.  There is no within-subject variance, so only the
\code{"ABE"} and \code{"descriptive"} frameworks apply, and the results may have no
groups other than \code{reference_col} (filter them first).
}
